{
	// glass-hour as an acid line, the short one. 26 bars, 44.98 s, five
	// tempos stepping up and one step back down. f sharp phrygian, as
	// glass-hour is.
	//
	// the plan. one voice carries it: a line of sixteenth notes on the
	// lead. most of its notes are the low f sharp (f#2) and its octave,
	// quiet, with gaps; glass-hour's own melody, an octave lower than it
	// was written, is the loud notes of the line, at the places in the
	// bar where glass-hour has them (one bar of glass-hour is one bar of
	// this, at twice its tempo and rising, so the tune goes by at a
	// little over twice its speed). under it a clock: a tick on each
	// beat and a metal tock between.
	//
	//   1-2   thin, 132 a minute. the line alone on f#2 with its octave,
	//         no melody yet, and the clock. bar 2 ends on g, the note a
	//         half step up that glass-hour leans on.
	//   3-6   ice. glass-hour bars 1 to 4, a loud note for each of its
	//         notes: c# f# c# | d g d | c# f# c# | b e b, over f#, g, f#
	//         and c in the line's low notes, as glass-hour's bass has.
	//   7-10  hairline, 136. a kick on every beat and a low bass off the
	//         beat. glass-hour bars 5 to 7 (c# e g c# e | d f# a | b d f#
	//         b d), the line with no gaps now. bar 10 is glass-hour's
	//         silent bar with its snare roll: the kick stops and the line
	//         climbs the scale two octaves in sixteenths, quiet to loud.
	//   11-14 it spreads, 140 then 144 at bar 13. bars 5 to 7 again with
	//         the melody back up at its own octave, where the filter is
	//         open; the bass rolls in sixteenths; chord stabs off the
	//         beat (the 64-bar piece's triads). bar 14: the kick doubles
	//         and the line leaps octaves up the scale.
	//   15-22 it breaks, 148, everything ff. glass-hour's crack, bars 9
	//         and 10, note for note an octave down: the eighth-note line
	//         with the low f# struck between each, then the sixteenth
	//         run as written. then the 64-bar piece's two answers to it
	//         (its bars 35, 36), the crack again, its rising run (bar
	//         37), and bar 22 falls down the scale and ends on one low
	//         blow half way through the bar.
	//   23-26 after, back to 132. the clock alone with glass-hour's last
	//         four bars: f# f# f#, the same on g, f# again, low and quiet,
	//         and it stops.
	//
	// accents: every melody note is f or ff; the filling notes are p in
	// bars 1 to 6, mp in 7 to 9, mf from 11. slides, to be written when
	// the machine takes them: from the low note into each melody note
	// that begins a group (bar 3 step 9, bar 4 step 5, bar 6 step 5), and
	// along the run in bar 10 and the leaps in bar 14.
